# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A set of spacetime Pauli checks is “good” if each check is valid, low weight, and effective.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang set ng spacetime Pauli checks ay “maganda” kung ang bawat check ay valid, low weight, at epektibo.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A check is comprised of a number of controlled Pauli rotations, $P$, placed on some wires in the circuit, $w$: $\\{(P_1,w_1), ..., (P_k,w_k)\\}$. For a given check, the controls lie on a single ancilla qubit, and the rotations occur on wires of a single target qubit. For Clifford circuits, a check is valid if its backpropagated product is a stabilizer of the state prepared by the ideal circuit: $\\prod_{i}B(P_i,w_i) \\in S$, where $B(P,w)$ is the backpropagator of $P$ from $w$ to the beginning of the circuit, and $S$ is the set of all stabilizers of the circuit.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang check ay binubuo ng ilang controlled Pauli rotation, $P$, na inilalagay sa ilang wire sa circuit, $w$: $\\{(P_1,w_1), ..., (P_k,w_k)\\}$. Para sa isang check, ang mga control ay nasa iisang ancilla qubit, at ang mga rotation ay nangyayari sa mga wire ng iisang target qubit. Para sa mga Clifford circuit, valid ang isang check kung ang backpropagated product nito ay stabilizer ng state na inihanda ng ideal na circuit: $\\prod_{i}B(P_i,w_i) \\in S$, kung saan ang $B(P,w)$ ay ang backpropagator ng $P$ mula $w$ hanggang sa simula ng circuit, at ang $S$ ay ang set ng lahat ng stabilizer ng circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A check is low weight if it requires few entangling gates to implement. The check-picking algorithm will favor checks that are low weight and provide the most effective error detection.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang check ay low weight kung kaunting entangling gate lang ang kailangan para ipatupad ito. Mas pipiliin ng check-picking algorithm ang mga check na low weight at nagbibigay ng pinakaepektibong error detection.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A check is effective if it captures much more error than it introduces. A Pauli check is comprised of a number of entangling gates and thus introduces some additional gate noise into the calculation. It is important to ensure each additional check brings some additional error detection capability. The effectiveness of a set of checks can be approximated by composing the Pauli errors that are uncovered by the checks into a postselected noise channel and calculating its impact. Minimizing the sampling overhead for implementing the uncovered inverse noise channel is a solid heuristic for selecting good checks, as it gives an indication of how much error the checks can’t detect. A slower but more realistic approach is to perform a Monte Carlo sampling from the noisy state and empirically compute the logical error rate of the postselected distribution. Both of these approaches are available as built-in cost functions in the `qiskit_paulice.add_pauli_checks` function.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang check ay epektibo kung mas marami itong nahuhuling error kaysa sa naidudulot nito. Ang isang Pauli check ay binubuo ng ilang entangling gate kaya nagdadagdag ito ng kaunting gate noise sa kalkulasyon. Mahalagang tiyakin na ang bawat karagdagang check ay may dagdag na kakayahang mag-detect ng error. Matatantya ang bisa ng isang set ng checks sa pamamagitan ng pagbuo ng mga Pauli error na natutuklasan ng mga check sa isang postselected noise channel at pagkalkula ng epekto nito. Ang pagpapaliit ng sampling overhead sa pagpapatupad ng natuklasang inverse noise channel ay solidong heuristic para sa pagpili ng magagandang check, dahil nagbibigay ito ng indikasyon kung gaano karaming error ang hindi kayang madetect ng mga check. Mas mabagal ngunit mas makatotohanang paraan ang magsagawa ng Monte Carlo sampling mula sa maingay na state at empirikal na kalkulahin ang logical error rate ng postselected distribution. Parehong available ang dalawang paraang ito bilang built-in na cost function sa function na `qiskit_paulice.add_pauli_checks`.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Postselecting samples based on syndrome data {#postselecting-samples-based-on-syndrome-data}"
msgstr "Pag-postselect ng mga sample batay sa syndrome data {#postselecting-samples-based-on-syndrome-data}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this package a check is implemented using entangling gates between one ancilla qubit and one target qubit. Each ancilla starts in $|0\\rangle$, so $Z_\\text{anc}$ stabilizes its input state. Forward-propagating $Z_\\text{anc}$ from the beginning of the ancilla through the entire checked circuit yields a Pauli operator on the output, which might be higher weight and extend into the payload circuit. The qubit indices on which this output operator has non-identity terms are called the check’s support, and the check passes if the bits, $b$, in the check’s support have even parity: $\\bigoplus_{i=1} b_i = 0$. A sample is kept if each check produces $0$ for its parity check.\n"
msgstr "Sa package na ito, ang isang check ay ipinapatupad gamit ang mga entangling gate sa pagitan ng isang ancilla qubit at isang target qubit. Nagsisimula ang bawat ancilla sa $|0\\rangle$, kaya ang $Z_\\text{anc}$ ay nag-istabilize sa input state nito. Ang forward-propagation ng $Z_\\text{anc}$ mula sa simula ng ancilla sa buong circuit na may checks ay nagbubunga ng Pauli operator sa output, na maaaring mas mataas ang weight at umabot sa payload circuit. Ang mga qubit index kung saan may non-identity na term ang output operator na ito ay tinatawag na support ng check, at pumapasa ang check kung ang mga bit, $b$, sa support ng check ay may even parity: $\\bigoplus_{i=1} b_i = 0$. Pinapanatili ang isang sample kung ang bawat check ay nagbubunga ng $0$ para sa parity check nito.\n"

